## Map Purpose

The map is the player's primary way of getting a larger spatial overview of ASCIIA BAY.

The normal gameplay camera remains at the fixed close view.

The map replaces the need for the player to zoom far out for navigation.


## Map Content

Show an overview of the entire currently playable island/world area.

Show:

- all main islands
- actual terrain/coastline shapes
- major placed world structures/assets useful for orientation
- player location
- relevant NPC locations

The map is for orientation.

It should not become a second fully detailed gameplay viewport.


## Derive the Map from Current World Data

Where technically practical, derive the map from the SAME source-of-truth world data used by the game.

Relevant data may include:

- terrain/world geometry
- coastline/island geometry
- terrain regions
- persistent placed world assets
- important structures
- current character positions

The purpose is to prevent the map and game world from becoming out of sync.

For example:

If I later move:

- a house
- a bridge
- a shop
- another important structure
- another relevant world asset

the map should reflect its updated position automatically.

Do NOT require me to manually maintain a separate static map image.


## Map Performance

Do NOT continuously run a second full-resolution instance of the entire game renderer in the background.

Prefer an architecture such as:

SEMI-STATIC WORLD MAP LAYER

Derived from:

- terrain
- coastline
- island shapes
- persistent relevant world assets

This can be:

- generated when the map opens
- cached
- invalidated/rebuilt when relevant world data changes

PLUS:

DYNAMIC CHARACTER OVERLAY

Derived from:

- current player position
- current NPC positions
- future moving markers

Choose the exact implementation based on the current architecture.

The important goals are:

- one source of truth
- automatic synchronization
- good performance
- no manually duplicated map layout


